{"schemaVersion":1,"albumId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b","editionNumber":1,"candidateContentHash":"fb361c9c20a2c980882a419b59e7deeee189ad8b121ee0ab04e647b9ee89420d","reviewer":{"identity":"codex-independent-evidence-reviewer","runId":"rank25-final-readonly-review-20260903","process":"codex exec --sandbox read-only"},"reviewedAt":"2026-09-03T17:41:54Z","decisions":[{"claimId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:1:fact-1","decisionId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:1:fact-1","decision":"supported","artifactIds":["ba7930eac96522e7426188eca64f73b653ec4854a52a05792f16a6faecb2fb82"],"sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/I_Feel_the_Earth_Move"],"rationale":"The artifact exists, the sourceRef URL matches the artifact canonicalUrl, and the offsets slice retainedText exactly to the extract. The retained context attributes the observation to James Perone and specifically says the accent at the end of \"tumbling\" produces a musical equivalent of a tumble."},{"claimId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:2:fact-1","decisionId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:2:fact-1","decision":"supported","artifactIds":["510f236365e3669e9be685d0be0a9f6d25bf88fa9750406314b2a4fd0bb18d70"],"sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/So_Far_Away_(Carole_King_song)"],"rationale":"The artifact exists, the sourceRef URL matches the artifact canonicalUrl, and the offsets slice retainedText exactly to the extract. The extract specifically identifies James Taylor on acoustic guitar, King on piano, Russ Kunkel on drums, Charles Larkey on bass guitar, and Curtis Amy on flute for the recording."},{"claimId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:3:fact-1","decisionId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:3:fact-1","decision":"supported","artifactIds":["4e7f193e89acb0b98319dc7f09e43f00ee8b9728f564719034842099a226dce4"],"sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/It%27s_Too_Late_(Carole_King_song)"],"rationale":"The artifact exists, the sourceRef URL matches the artifact canonicalUrl, and the offsets slice retainedText exactly to the extract. The retained context names Danny Kortchmar and Curtis Amy, then states that Kortchmar and Amy each have an instrumental solo on the song."},{"claimId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:5:fact-1","decisionId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:5:fact-1","decision":"supported","artifactIds":["f3a616f9b5d7455a99279f00be4d34101fdb0f69a853de912b867d18f8b151e1"],"sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beautiful_(Carole_King_song)"],"rationale":"The artifact exists, the sourceRef URL matches the artifact canonicalUrl, and the offsets slice retainedText exactly to the extract. The source attributes the statement to Carole King and says she did not consciously try to write \"Beautiful\" because it came to her spontaneously."},{"claimId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:7:fact-1","decisionId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:7:fact-1","decision":"supported","artifactIds":["f28c84958930e67433a6c52b0aad8b9930506e1e3844776c378bc45772b74a94"],"sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/You%27ve_Got_a_Friend"],"rationale":"The artifact exists, the sourceRef URL matches the artifact canonicalUrl, and the offsets slice retainedText exactly to the extract. The extract says that according to Taylor, King told him the song responded to a line in his earlier song \"Fire and Rain.\""},{"claimId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:8:fact-1","decisionId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:8:fact-1","decision":"supported","artifactIds":["4c0ac35704fc4c1ecf23fd51e37c504b16159c5bbcd2c6ffe631ea5ecefd3672"],"sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Where_You_Lead"],"rationale":"The artifact exists, the sourceRef URL matches the artifact canonicalUrl, and the offsets slice retainedText exactly to the extract. The extract specifically says King had written the music and majority of the lyric before soliciting Stern's help with the bridge in exchange for co-writing credit."},{"claimId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:9:fact-1","decisionId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:9:fact-1","decision":"supported","artifactIds":["3268dba4222380cba110474e432ed6a47636634cc1352791457645511fffcd47"],"sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Will_You_Love_Me_Tomorrow"],"rationale":"The artifact exists, the sourceRef URL matches the artifact canonicalUrl, and the offsets slice retainedText exactly to the extract. The extract states that King's 1971 Tapestry version used Joni Mitchell and James Taylor for background vocals on separate audio channels."},{"claimId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:10:fact-1","decisionId":"025-carole-king-tapestry-b29b056b:1:10:fact-1","decision":"supported","artifactIds":["db76a67da1f0f16297133757971b6e8049c6c36c77076382b78e52b78097e63b"],"sourceUrls":["https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Smackwater_Jack_(song)"],"rationale":"The artifact exists, the sourceRef URL matches the artifact canonicalUrl, and the offsets slice retainedText exactly to the extract. The retained context says the lyrics tell the story of a confrontation between the outlaw Smackwater Jack and Big Jim the Chief, which directly supports the track-specific claim."}]}